 1 big thing: Bill Gross is building an AI factory for startups 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Bill Gross has spent 30 years turning problems, technologies and market shifts into companies — and now he is trying to turn that process into a machine.

 Why it matters: In a moment when AI is mostly framed as a force that eliminates jobs, Gross is betting it can help create entrepreneurs and companies.

 The big picture: Gross is launching Idealab.ai, a platform that has analyzed more than 17 million sources and generated 1.2 million potential businesses.

 He plans to give all the ideas away — and even help with financing. How it works: Idealab.ai lets individuals sort through the ideas and narrow them based on what matters to them.

 They can filter by field of interest, geography, size of market opportunity, required capital, team size, degree of automation and other metrics. A solo founder looking for a local business can search differently from a university looking to commercialize research or a company exploring new uses for its patents. Each idea comes with a business brief, potential customers, competitors, first steps and reasons it might fail. Catch up quick: Gross says the idea began with an article about a global copper shortage.

 His insight was that almost every source of information contains either a problem worth solving or an emerging capability that could help solve it. Those include news stories, research papers, patents, company filings and community discussions. Gross built a "Periodic Table of Business Functions," covering 118 jobs involved in running a company. "My methodology is to divide everything into a possible problem or a possible enabler to solve a problem," Gross told me. Between the lines: In many ways, this is Gross trying to turn his life's work into an automated process. 

 Gross founded Idealab in the 1990s and says Idealab and Idealab Studio have launched more than 175 companies, including GoTo.com, Citysearch and Tickets.com. Yes, but: Gross says AI shouldn't make every decision, especially strategic choices, such as how much risk to take.

 "AI is not going to make that one for you," he said. The intrigue: Idealab.ai will be a real-world experiment in how much success comes from the idea — versus the entrepreneur's zeal, execution and personal network.

 A strong concept may still go nowhere if the founder cannot sell it, recruit people, raise money or get an introduction to the right customer. And a mediocre idea may work if the person pursuing it has unusual persistence and access. Zoom out: Gross wants Idealab.ai to become an ecosystem, not just a database.

 He is courting universities, incubators and accelerators, as well as businesses that can provide the tools and services founders need to build companies. Cloudflare is the first announced partner, offering $100,000 in credits to companies started through the platform. Gross hopes it will be the first of many such partnerships. The bottom line: Gross, who remains CEO of licensing and revenue sharing company ProRata.ai , says he is done starting companies himself.

 Idealab let him start 12 companies at once. His ambition now is to help support 12,000. 
 
